==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fro|consigner}} or directly from {{der|en|la|cōnsignō||furnish with a seal}}, from {{m|la|con-}} + {{m|la|signō||mark, sign}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/kənˈsaɪn/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|aɪn|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|con|sign|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|business}} To transfer to the custody of, usually for sale, transport, or [[safekeeping]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To entrust to the care of another.
#* {{quote-book|year=1726|author={{w|Alexander Pope}}, {{w|William Broome}}, and {{w|Elijah Fenton}}, transl.|title={{w|Odyssey}}|type=translation|by={{w|Homer}}|edition=new|volume=I|location=London|publisher={{w|T. Longman}} et al.|year_published=1796|section=book III|lines=332–5|pages=147–8|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=2iQ-AAAAcAAJ&pg=PA148|passage=For virtue’s image yet poſſeſt her mind, / Taught by a maſter of the tuneful kind : / Atrides, parting for the Trojan war, / '''Conſign’d''' the youthful conſort to his care.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To send to a final destination.
#: ''to '''consign''' the body to the grave''
#* {{quote-book|date=1707-04-7|author={{w|Francis Atterbury}}|chapter=A Spittal-Sermon Preach’d at St. Bridget’s Church, before the Right Honourable the Lord-Mayor, &c.|title=Sermons and Discourses on Several Subjects and Occasions|edition=5th|volume=II|location=London|publisher=T. Woodward and C. Davis|year_published=1740|page=151|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=YbGHuz3du4oC&pg=RA1-PA151|passage=And this remarkable Property of Love will ſuggeſt to us one Reaſon, why Acts of Charity ſhall be enquir’d after ſo particularly, at the Day of general Account ; becauſe Good Men are then to be '''conſign’d''' over to another State, a State of everlaſting Love and Charity{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal
|date=December 15, 2011
|author=Felicity Cloake
|title=How to cook the perfect nut roast
|work=Guardian
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/lifeandstyle/wordofmouth/2011/dec/15/how-cook-perfect-nut-roast
|page=
|passage=If there's such a thing as pariah food – a recipe shunned by mainstream menus, mocked to near extinction and '''consigned''' to niche hinterlands for evermore – then the nut roast, a dish whose very name has become a watchword for sawdusty disappointment, is surely a strong contender.}}
# To assign; to devote; to set apart.
#* {{quote-book|year=a. 1700|author={{w|John Dryden}}|chapter=Dedication|title=The British Poets|volume=XI|location=Edinburgh|publisher=A. Kincaid and W. Creech, and J. Balfour|year_published=1773|page=13|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=JhQUAAAAQAAJ&pg=PP17|passage=The French commander, charmed with the greatneſs of your ſoul, accordingly '''conſign’d''' it [a donation] to the uſe for which it was intended by the donor{{...}}}}
# To stamp or impress; to affect.
#* {{quote-book|year=1650|author={{w|Jeremy Taylor}}|chapter=Devotions for ordinary days|title=The Rule and Exercises of Holy Living|edition=19th|location=London|publisher=J. Hepinstall|year_published=1703|page=44|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_dUHAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA44|passage=Ennoble my ſoul with great degrees of love to thee, and '''conſign''' my ſpirit with great fear, religion and veneration of thy holy name and laws{{...}}}}

